  ("complications discussed above. Particularly in the case of paragraphs, the order of the" 0.0981 0.9024 0.4250 0.4389 0.4389 0.0138 0.0065 0.2569 0.1303)
  ("elements is preserved and every element has a translation, cf. Fig. 1. What is more," 0.0981 0.9022 0.4425 0.4563 0.4563 0.0138 0.0084 0.2951 0.0825)
  ("we assume that the translator can join two or more source paragraphs into one trans-" 0.0981 0.9025 0.4599 0.4738 0.4738 0.0138 0.0073 0.2631 0.0267)
  ("lated paragraph or split one source paragraph into several translated paragraphs, as in" 0.0981 0.9025 0.4774 0.4912 0.4912 0.0138 0.0068 0.2198 0.0451)
  ("Fig. 4, cf. Fig. 2 (a) and Fig. 3, but cannot re-arrange the sentences in the paragraphs" 0.0981 0.9023 0.4948 0.5087 0.5087 0.0138 0.0070 0.2465 0.0365)
  ("in such a way that, say, a final part of a paragraph be translated as a beginning of" 0.0981 0.9028 0.5123 0.5261 0.5261 0.0138 0.0090 0.2005 0.0181)
  ("another paragraph, as in Fig. 5, which would lead to patterns such as those shown in" 0.0981 0.9026 0.5296 0.5434 0.5434 0.0138 0.0074 0.2570 0.0694)
  ("Fig. 2 (b, c). Though these assumptions are not completely true to life, they signifi-" 0.0981 0.9026 0.5470 0.5608 0.5607 0.0138 0.0082 0.2687 0.0367)
  ("cantly simplify formalization of the task and the algorithm." 0.0981 0.6476 0.5645 0.5783 0.5783 0.0138 0.0059 0.3269 0.0567)
  ("Thus, we define the paragraph-level bilingual text alignment task as the problem of" 0.1246 0.9027 0.5819 0.5958 0.5958 0.0138 0.0062 0.2979 0.0521)
  ("constructing a bipartite hypergraph (cf. Fig. 2 (c)) whose vertices are the paragraphs" 0.0981 0.9024 0.5994 0.6132 0.6132 0.0138 0.0079 0.2272 0.1148)
  ("of the texts in the two languages, respectively, and whose hyperarcs—standing for the" 0.0981 0.9024 0.6168 0.6307 0.6307 0.0138 0.0062 0.3061 0.0194)
  ("sets of vertices to be mutual translations—satisfy the following conditions:" 0.0981 0.7950 0.6341 0.6479 0.6479 0.0138 0.0059 0.3322 0.0347)
  ("- Every vertex is incident to an arc, i.e., no paragraph disappears or appears from" 0.0981 0.9028 0.6607 0.6745 0.6745 0.0138 0.0089 0.2056 0.0139)
  ("nothing in the translation process;" 0.1246 0.4398 0.6781 0.6919 0.6919 0.0138 0.0057 0.2582 0.0710)
  ("- At least one side of each arc has only one end, i.e., an arc can connect element to" 0.0981 0.9026 0.6956 0.7094 0.7094 0.0138 0.0073 0.2528 0.0139)
  ("element, element to group, group to element, but not group to group;" 0.1246 0.7641 0.7130 0.7268 0.7268 0.0138 0.0060 0.3193 0.0792)
  ("- The ends of each arc are contiguous, i.e., a group of paragraphs that is the transla-" 0.0981 0.9026 0.7303 0.7441 0.7441 0.0138 0.0070 0.2374 0.0139)
  ("tion of a paragraph in the other language occupy a contiguous run of the text;" 0.1246 0.8424 0.7477 0.7616 0.7616 0.0138 0.0058 0.2626 0.0361)
  ("- The arcs are not crossing, i.e., the order of paragraphs is preserved in translation." 0.0981 0.8757 0.7652 0.7790 0.7790 0.0138 0.0059 0.2643 0.0139)
  ("The (sets of) paragraphs that are translations of each other are similar in the sense" 0.1246 0.9027 0.7917 0.8056 0.8054 0.0138 0.0071 0.2363 0.0362)
  ("of Section 0 below. This similarity measure can be assigned to the hyperarc connect-" 0.0981 0.9026 0.8092 0.8230 0.8230 0.0138 0.0071 0.2694 0.0194)
  ("ing the paragraphs; we call this value the weight of the hyperarc. Our hypothesis is" 0.0981 0.9025 0.8267 0.8405 0.8403 0.0138 0.0085 0.2371 0.0295)
  ("that the total weight of all hyperarcs gives the quality of a particular alignment. With" 0.0981 0.9024 0.8439 0.8578 0.8578 0.0138 0.0070 0.3099 0.0346)
  ("this, the task is reduced to finding, among all possible hypergraphs satisfying the" 0.0981 0.9024 0.8614 0.8752 0.8752 0.0138 0.0103 0.2240 0.0391)
  ("above conditions, the one with the maximum total weight of its hyperarcs." 0.0981 0.7887 0.8788 0.8927 0.8927 0.0138 0.0059 0.3299 0.0555))
  (5
  ("828" 0.0981 0.1297 0.0439 0.0564 0.0564 0.0125 nil 0.0000 0.0316)
  ("A. Gelbukh and G. Sidorov" 0.1770 0.4075 0.0439 0.0564 0.0564 0.0125 0.0054 0.3382 0.0200)
  ("3 Distance Measure" 0.0981 0.3460 0.0880 0.1044 0.1044 0.0164 0.0209 0.3424 0.0140)
  ("To assign the weight to a hyperarc as described in Section 0 above, we need to calcu-" 0.0981 0.9026 0.1261 0.1399 0.1399 0.0138 0.0066 0.2595 0.0261)
  ("late the similarity between two sets of paragraphs (in our algorithm at least one of the" 0.0981 0.9024 0.1436 0.1574 0.1574 0.0138 0.0064 0.3036 0.0334)
  ("two sets consists of only one paragraph). We define it as the similarity between the" 0.0981 0.9023 0.1610 0.1748 0.1748 0.0138 0.0082 0.2845 0.0345)
  ("two texts that are obtained by concatenation of the corresponding paragraphs." 0.0981 0.8189 0.1785 0.1923 0.1923 0.0138 0.0059 0.2548 0.0345)
  ("3.1 Baseline Distance Measure" 0.0981 0.4105 0.2145 0.2281 0.2281 0.0136 0.0060 0.3507 0.0291)
  ("Common sense suggests that the corresponding pieces of texts are located at ap-" 0.0981 0.9029 0.2450 0.2588 0.2588 0.0138 0.0109 0.2159 0.0864)
  ("proximately the relative same distance from the beginning of the whole text. We de-" 0.0981 0.9028 0.2625 0.2763 0.2763 0.0138 0.0076 0.3081 0.1122)
  ("fine the baseline distance between two pieces of text, T_{A} in the language A and T_{B} in" 0.0981 0.9028 0.2799 0.2949 0.2936 0.0138 0.0073 0.2589 0.0360)
  ("the language B, as follows:" 0.0981 0.3490 0.2974 0.3112 0.3110 0.0138 0.0059 0.3028 0.0282)
  ("Distance(T_{A}, T_{B}) = |start(T_{A}) – start(T_{B})| + |end(T_{A}) – end(T_{B})|," 0.1799 0.7497 0.3239 0.3389 0.3378 0.0138 0.0057 0.3302 0.1185)
  ("(1)" 0.8750 0.9023 0.3239 0.3378 0.3378 0.0138 nil 0.1984 0.0272)
  ("where start(T_{X}) is the relative position of the first word of the text T_{X} measured in" 0.0981 0.9025 0.3505 0.3654 0.3641 0.0138 0.0092 0.2474 0.0565)
  ("percentage of the total number of words in the text in the corresponding language, and" 0.0981 0.9021 0.3678 0.3816 0.3816 0.0138 0.0060 0.2797 0.1005)
  ("similarly for end(T_{X}). We could also use the position of the paragraph instead of word" 0.0981 0.9025 0.3852 0.4001 0.3990 0.0138 0.0065 0.3060 0.0828)
  ("as percentage of the total number of paragraphs, but the measure based on word" 0.0981 0.9025 0.4027 0.4165 0.4165 0.0138 0.0107 0.2114 0.0194)
  ("counts has been reported as better than the one based on paragraph counts, which" 0.0981 0.9026 0.4201 0.4339 0.4339 0.0138 0.0096 0.1814 0.0607)
  ("agrees with our own observations." 0.0981 0.4162 0.4376 0.4514 0.4514 0.0138 0.0059 0.2831 0.0594)
  ("3.2 Proposed Distance Measure" 0.0981 0.4209 0.4736 0.4872 0.4872 0.0136 0.0059 0.3200 0.0291)
  ("We define the similarity between two texts in different languages as the number of" 0.0981 0.9026 0.5041 0.5179 0.5179 0.0138 0.0086 0.2893 0.0323)
  ("words in both texts that are not mutual translations of each other 12. For this, we first" 0.0981 0.9026 0.5216 0.5354 0.5354 0.0138 0.0065 0.2930 0.0567)
  ("define which words are such translations." 0.0981 0.4825 0.5390 0.5528 0.5528 0.0138 0.0059 0.2796 0.0576)
  ("1. Set T_{X}, := the shortest one of T_{A} and T_{B}; T_{Y} := the longest" 0.2298 0.7916 0.5656 0.5805 0.5792 0.0138 0.0059 0.3076 0.0175)
  ("2. Set translations := 0" 0.2298 0.4486 0.5830 0.5968 0.5968 0.0138 0.0059 0.3203 0.0175)
  ("3. for each word token w in T_{X}" 0.2298 0.5226 0.6003 0.6152 0.6139 0.0138 0.0059 0.3246 0.0175)
  ("4." 0.2298 0.2473 0.6178 0.6316 0.6316 0.0138 nil nil 0.0175)
  ("if any of its translations D_{XY} (w) is found in T_{Y}" 0.2959 0.7252 0.6178 0.6327 0.6314 0.0138 0.0058 0.3150 0.0141)
  ("5." 0.2298 0.2473 0.6352 0.6490 0.6490 0.0138 nil nil 0.0175)
  ("increase translations by 1" 0.3289 0.5644 0.6352 0.6490 0.6490 0.0138 0.0058 0.2462 0.0759)
  ("where D_{XY} (w) is a function returning a set of the dictionary translations of the word" 0.0981 0.9026 0.6618 0.6767 0.6756 0.0138 0.0075 0.2641 0.0566)
  ("w. Then the number of word tokens without translation in both paragraphs, under the" 0.0981 0.9024 0.6792 0.6930 0.6928 0.0138 0.0070 0.2810 0.0211)
  ("hypothesis that these two paragraphs correspond to each other, is:" 0.0981 0.7088 0.6967 0.7105 0.7105 0.0138 0.0059 0.2545 0.0992)
  ("Distance(T_{A}, T_{B}) = | T_{A} ​| + | T_{B} ​| – 2 × translations." 0.2340 0.6959 0.7196 0.7393 0.7379 0.0138 0.0059 0.3492 0.1169)
  ("(2)" 0.8750 0.9022 0.7236 0.7374 0.7374 0.0138 nil 0.1982 0.0272)
  ("The cost of an alignment hypothesis is the total number of words in both texts that" 0.1246 0.9027 0.7508 0.7647 0.7647 0.0138 0.0067 0.2911 0.0362)
  ("are left without translation under this hypothesis. Note that under different hypotheses" 0.0981 0.9026 0.7681 0.7819 0.7819 0.0138 0.0062 0.2758 0.0285)
  ("this number is different: here we consider two word tokens to be translations of each" 0.0981 0.9026 0.7856 0.7994 0.7994 0.0138 0.0072 0.2705 0.0334)
  ("other if both of the following conditions hold: (a) they are dictionary translations (as" 0.0981 0.9025 0.8030 0.8168 0.8168 0.0138 0.0074 0.2493 0.0476)
  ("word types) and (b) the paragraphs where they occur are supposed to be aligned." 0.0981 0.8470 0.8205 0.8343 0.8343 0.0138 0.0059 0.2590 0.0476)
  ("Note that we represent the texts as vectors of word frequencies, ignoring the order" 0.1246 0.9025 0.8379 0.8518 0.8518 0.0138 0.0070 0.2598 0.0451)
  ("of the words. In particular, concatenation of the paragraphs into text pieces is per-" 0.0981 0.9025 0.8552 0.8690 0.8690 0.0138 0.0091 0.2128 0.0194)
  ("formed simply as summation of the corresponding vectors." 0.0981 0.6454 0.8727 0.8865 0.8865 0.0138 0.0058 0.3162 0.0669))
  (6
  ("Alignment of Paragraphs in Bilingual Texts" 0.4564 0.8233 0.0439 0.0564 0.0564 0.0125 0.0053 0.3204 0.0894)
  ("829" 0.8706 0.9022 0.0439 0.0564 0.0564 0.0125 nil 0.0000 0.0316)
  ("The above algorithm for calculating the number of translations has a drawback: in" 0.1246 0.9028 0.0870 0.1008 0.1008 0.0138 0.0070 0.2714 0.0362)
  ("line 828, the same word in T_{Y} can potentially be counted twice, as in the English sen-" 0.0981 0.9024 0.1045 0.1194 0.1181 0.0138 0.0067 0.2870 0.0346)
  ("tence T_{A} = “The bank is at the French border” and Spanish sentence T_{B} = “Juan vive a" 0.0981 0.9026 0.1219 0.1369 0.1356 0.0138 0.0059 0.2538 0.0488)
  ("la orilla de la ciudad” ‘John lives at the border of the city.’ However, addressing this" 0.0981 0.9023 0.1394 0.1532 0.1530 0.0138 0.0068 0.2688 0.0180)
  ("problem would lead to a more complicated and computationally more expensive algo-" 0.0981 0.9025 0.1568 0.1707 0.1707 0.0138 0.0062 0.3079 0.0775)
  ("rithm, which we may consider in our future work." 0.0981 0.5617 0.1741 0.1879 0.1879 0.0138 0.0060 0.3441 0.0558)
  ("Recall that in our formalization of the task we select the optimal hypergraph out of" 0.1246 0.9026 0.1916 0.2054 0.2054 0.0138 0.0065 0.2972 0.0592)
  ("hypergraphs with different number of arcs. This leads to that the algorithm would" 0.0981 0.9022 0.2090 0.2228 0.2228 0.0138 0.0097 0.2584 0.1146)
  ("usually prefer a smaller number of hyperarcs: in an extreme case it might tend to align" 0.0981 0.9031 0.2265 0.2403 0.2403 0.0138 0.0060 0.3142 0.0672)
  ("the firs paragraph of A with all but one paragraphs of B, and the rest of A with the last" 0.0981 0.9024 0.2439 0.2578 0.2576 0.0138 0.0062 0.2918 0.0281)
  ("paragraph of B, which gives only two hyperarcs. However, the specific measure we" 0.0981 0.9023 0.2614 0.2752 0.2750 0.0138 0.0082 0.2648 0.0931)
  ("suggest here is linear in the sense that it does not depend on the number of arcs: the" 0.0981 0.9024 0.2787 0.2925 0.2925 0.0138 0.0075 0.2292 0.0697)
  ("cost is proportionally greater for the hyperarcs that align more paragraphs with one." 0.0981 0.9026 0.2961 0.3099 0.3099 0.0138 0.0082 0.2340 0.0374)
  ("Thus with this particular measure the algorithm is not biased towards a smaller num-" 0.0981 0.9023 0.3136 0.3274 0.3274 0.0138 0.0071 0.3106 0.0464)
  ("ber of larger pieces being aligned. Note that our experiments show (see Table 2) that" 0.0981 0.9024 0.3310 0.3448 0.3448 0.0138 0.0070 0.2665 0.0297)
  ("our baseline measure suffers from such a bias." 0.0981 0.5281 0.3485 0.3623 0.3623 0.0138 0.0059 0.2883 0.0308)
  ("4 Algorithm" 0.0981 0.2570 0.3941 0.4106 0.4106 0.0164 0.0209 0.3203 0.0140)
  ("To find the exact optimal alignment, we apply a dynamic programming algorithm. It" 0.0981 0.9026 0.4323 0.4461 0.4461 0.0138 0.0073 0.3213 0.0261)
  ("uses a (N_{A} + 1) × (N_{B} + 1) chart shown in Fig. 6, where N_{X} is the number of para-" 0.0981 0.9025 0.4462 0.4658 0.4645 0.0138 0.0090 0.2616 0.0400)
  ("graphs in the text in the language X." 0.0981 0.4326 0.4681 0.4819 0.4818 0.0138 0.0058 0.2758 0.0617)
  ("The algorithm works as follows. First, the chart is filled in:" 0.1246 0.6734 0.4856 0.4994 0.4994 0.0138 0.0059 0.3350 0.0362)
  ("a_{00} := 0, a_{i0} := –∞, a_{0j} := –∞ for all i, j > 0." 0.2641 0.6547 0.5134 0.5284 0.5270 0.0138 0.0059 0.3852 0.0268)
  ("for i from 1 to N_{A} do" 0.2641 0.4554 0.5307 0.5456 0.5443 0.0138 0.0060 0.3849 0.0270)
  ("for j from 1 to N_{B} do" 0.2960 0.4872 0.5481 0.5630 0.5618 0.0138 0.0060 0.3849 0.0270)
  ("a_{ij} := min (a_{xy} + Distance (T_{A} [x + 1 .. i], T_{B} [y + 1 .. j]))" 0.3289 0.8403 0.5656 0.5805 0.5792 0.0138 0.0059 0.3926 0.0201)
  ("Language A" 0.2887 0.3099 0.7362 0.8090 0.8090 0.0092 -0.0212 nil 0.0212)
